window.docContents[186].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity--Operational-Boundary-Geometry":{"contents":"Boundary regularity needs more than an abstract smooth-boundary label: the\nproof differentiates flattening maps, transfers weak derivatives, and sums\nlocalized estimates through a finite partition of unity. The operational\natlases record exactly that data, with concrete instances for the\ninterval and every positive-dimensional Euclidean unit ball.\n\nThis reuses the geometry of\nSobolev extension, but asks it to do more.\nExtension continues a function whose first derivatives are already controlled.\nBoundary regularity must obtain an additional derivative from the equation.\nFlattening transforms the coefficients as well as the function, and\ndifferentiating that transformed equation requires extra chart derivatives.\nWe use explicit atlases so that this extra cost is part of the\nhypotheses, even on the familiar interval and ball.\n\nA  boundary atlas consists of a finite extension atlas whose flattening\ncharts and inverse charts are twice continuously differentiable.\n\nThe concrete atlases follow Evans's boundary-flattening argument on\npp. 334–340 and start from a finite cover of the boundary.\n\nA finite boundary cover consists of bounded open neighborhoods  whose\nunion contains the frontier  of a bounded open domain.\nAdding  as the interior member gives a finite open cover of\n. Mathlib's smooth partition-of-unity theorem then\ncanonically supplies compactly supported interior and boundary test functions\nsubordinate to those sets and summing to one on .\n\nA cover alone carries no analytic information about the boundary. The\nflattening charts add it: each boundary patch receives a map identifying the\ndomain locally with the upper half-space, and the differentiability class of\nthese maps is what distinguishes the  extension atlas from the \nregularity atlas.\n\nAfter the subordinate cutoffs are fixed, one attaches a flattening chart to\neach boundary neighborhood. Only the compact support of the corresponding\ncutoff must lie in the chart source. Globally represented  charts\nproduce an extension atlas, while  charts produce the operational\nboundary atlas used by elliptic regularity.\n\nThe first instance is the interval. In one dimension the boundary consists\nof two points, and affine maps flatten it.\n\nFor , the affine endpoint maps\n and  identify neighborhoods of the two\nboundary points with neighborhoods in the upper half-line .\nBoth maps are affine with affine inverses, hence smooth to every finite\norder. Together with an interior patch and a finite subordinate\npartition of unity, they furnish the operational  boundary atlas used\nby the global elliptic and parabolic regularity theorems.\n\nIn higher dimensions the unit ball is the model domain, and its atlas is\nassembled in two stages. The first stage is the cover: finitely many signed\ncoordinate caps containing the sphere, together with a subordinate partition\nof unity; each cap is later flattened by a graph chart.\n\nIn every positive dimension, the Euclidean unit ball has a finite cover by\n signed coordinate caps together with a smooth subordinate partition of\nunity. The interior and boundary cutoffs have compact support in their\nassigned sets and sum exactly to one on the ball. Its global defining\nfunction  is smooth, is positive exactly in the\nball, and has nonzero radial differential on the unit sphere.The caps cover the sphere: a point of the unit sphere is nonzero, so at least\none coordinate is either positive or negative, and the identity\n shows that the remaining tangential coordinates have\nsquared norm strictly less than one, placing the point in the corresponding\nsigned cap. Together with the open ball, these finitely many caps cover the\nclosed ball. Mathlib's smooth partition-of-unity theorem supplies the cutoffs,\nand boundedness of the cover sets makes their supports compact.\n\nOn each cap the sphere is the graph of a height function over the tangential\ncoordinates, and subtracting or adding that height in the normal coordinate\nflattens the cap. The constructor requires chart representatives\ndifferentiable on all of , so the local height\n is replaced by a globally smooth function\nagreeing with it on a slightly smaller tangential cylinder.\n\nFor a globally smooth height , the triangular\nmapsflatten the region below the graph of  and the region above the graph\nof , respectively, to the standard upper half-space. Their forward and\ninverse maps retain every finite differentiability order, and restriction to\na bounded open source gives the bounded partial charts required by the\noperational atlas. On every smaller\ntangential cylinder of the unit sphere, we also construct a global\n height agreeing with .\n\nThe flattening maps preserve every tangential coordinate and replace the\nnormal coordinate by  or . Direct coordinate\ncalculations give their inverses. The inequalities  and\n, respectively, are equivalent to positivity of the flattened\nnormal coordinate. Componentwise\ncalculus proves the required differentiability. Restricting each global\nhomeomorphism to a bounded open source gives a bounded partial chart because\nthe image of the compact source closure is bounded. For the sphere, the local\nheight  is smooth on the region where its\nradicand is positive; Mathlib's relative smooth-extension theorem produces a\nglobally smooth function agreeing with it on any strictly smaller closed\ntangential cylinder.\n\nFeeding the cap cover and the graph charts into the constructor gives the\nconcrete  atlas on which the global regularity\ntheorems can be instantiated for the unit ball in every positive dimension.\n\nFor every , quantitative signed caps, coordinate permutations,\nglobally smooth extensions of\n, and triangular graph-flattening maps\nassemble into a finite operational  boundary atlas for\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators\u0009Interior and Boundary Regularity","header":"5.6.1.1. Operational Boundary Geometry","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity--Operational-Boundary-Geometry"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles--Nonlinear-heat-profiles":{"contents":"For a real exponent  and an open time set , this structure records a\nnonnegative classical solution ofon . Its regularity scope is joint spacetime \nfor  together with  regularity of each spatial slice. Time\ndifferentiability is derived from the joint  field; joint \nregularity is not required. The equation is imposed only at times in , so\na solution on  says nothing at .\n\nA profile must satisfy the stated regularity, sign and equation conditions.\nThe Type I exponents make , , and  all scale by the same power of\n.\n\nFor the Type I ansatzThe reduced elliptic residual isand the Type I profile equation says exactly that this residual vanishes\npointwise. This is the standard Type I reduction used by Giga–Kohn, Comm.\nPure Appl. Math. 38 (1985), DOI 10.1002/cpa.3160380304; the Lean statements\nare the exact substitution identities.\n\nThe definition only names the two residuals. Substituting the ansatz into the\nequation relates them.\n\nSuppose , , and the profile is  and nonnegative at the\ndisplayed similarity coordinate. Then the exact residual identity isThe two balance laws are visible in the formal proof:  aligns the\nLaplacian scale and  aligns the reaction scale. Profile\nnonnegativity is used only to split the real power of the amplitude-profile\nproduct.\n\nWrite , , and\n. The backward similarity chain rules give\n and\n, because two spatial scale factors produce\n. Nonnegativity permits\n, and . Factoring\n leaves the stated profile residual.\n\nSince the prefactor  is strictly positive for\n, the residual identity upgrades at once to a profile equivalence.\n\nUnder the factorization hypotheses, the full parabolic residual vanishes at\n and  if and only if the reduced profile residual\nvanishes at the corresponding similarity coordinate. Consequently, the ansatz\nof a nonnegative  profile with  and zero Type I profile residual\neverywhere satisfiespointwise throughout the pre-singular cylinder.\n\nThe factorization writes the full residual as the profile residual multiplied\nby . For , this prefactor is strictly positive\nand hence nonzero. Therefore the product vanishes exactly when the profile\nresidual at the corresponding similarity point vanishes. Applying this\npointwise to the Type I profile equation hypothesis proves the equation\neverywhere.\n\nThe pointwise equation is one field of the classical-solution record. The\nothers, regularity and sign, are inherited from the profile because the\namplitude and the similarity scale are smooth and positive before .\nAssembling all of them needs the global hypotheses, nonnegativity everywhere\nand , that the pointwise identity did not.\n\nIf the Type I profile residual vanishes everywhere, then under the same\nregularity and sign assumptions, with , the ansatz is a\nnonnegative pointwise classical solution on\n. For a bounded profile with a nonzero value,\nthe Type I blow-up theorem separately gives finite spatial slices and\nsupremum-norm blow-up at .Under the same regularity and sign hypotheses, the companion theorem\non the profile-solution equivalence\nalso proves the converse: a Type I ansatz is a pointwise classical solution\non the full pre-singular interval exactly when its profile residual vanishes.\n\nOn , the amplitude and similarity scale are smooth and positive.\nComposition with the  profile gives joint  regularity and\n regularity of each spatial slice; profile nonnegativity and the\npositive amplitude give . The Type I profile equation and the\nzero-residual equivalence supply  at every point. These\nfields assemble the classical-solution structure. The converse evaluates the\nsame nonzero factorization.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Nonlinear Heat Singularity Profiles","header":"6.7.1.6.3. Nonlinear heat profiles","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles--Nonlinear-heat-profiles"}});